#!/usr/bin/env etcs
#IMPORT ACE_ROOT/modules

# A 3D scene on a window, driven by w/a/s/d, ending detached in the shell.
#
#     ./bin/etcs scripts/render_scene3d.etcs
#
# and then, with the window focused, hold W/S/A/D/Q/E and move the mouse to
# look. From the prompt, while it is still running:
#
#     Root> RenderProvider                       # read the RIDs back
#     Root> eye.LookAt(0.0, 12.0, -6.0, 0.0, 0.0, 6.0)   # move the eye
#     Root> tower_far.SetColor(1.0, 0.2, 0.2, 1.0)       # repaint one box
#     Root> block.SetVisible(0)                          # hide the occluder
#     Root> world.DepthAt(@eye, 512, 384)                # how far is the centre
#     Root> world.Order()                                # energy, heat, ticks
#     Root> main.CaptureMouse(0)                         # or just press Ctrl+Tab
#     Root> world.SetSensitivity(0.5)                    # halve the look rate
#     Root> world.Look()                                 # angles, rate, lens
#
# Every one of those takes effect on the next frame, because the frame edge
# re-walks the tree rather than replaying a list of calls (Surface.Compose,
# below). That is what "drop into the shell and modify the live scene" means
# here, and it is the same shell the 2D scene scripts already hand you.
#
# THE STRUCTURE, four things, each doing one job:
#
#   world  a Scene3D  -- geometry, and the thing w/a/s/d translates
#   eye    a Camera3D -- the image plane; a Drawable2D that a scene fills
#   stage  a compositor -- where the 3D view and any 2D UI merge
#   view   a Surface  -- the window's presentable surface
#
# and the seam between them is one call each way: the camera names the scene by
# RID, the compositor holds the camera as an ordinary 2D child, and the surface
# is handed the compositor as the root to walk. Nothing anywhere says "this one
# is the 3D one" -- ontology/Camera.h on why that is the whole point.
#
# THREAD LEDGER -- five, the same five render_script_streamed.etcs has, with
# the input consumer doing more than logging:
#
#   1. this script's thread   main.Run()               poll-free idle block
#   2. pool worker            Window.ProduceEvents     poll loop + event source
#   3. detached thread        Scene3D.ConsumeInput     key bitset + motion tick
#   4. pool worker            Surface.ProduceFrames    frame clock
#   5. detached thread        Surface.ConsumeFrames    walk, record, present

spawn RenderProvider::Instance gpu
gpu.Create()

# Create before Run: the Surface needs a live window to take its handle and
# extent from, and Run would block before we could attach one. Run's own
# CreateWindow is idempotent, so this order is safe and Run just enters the
# idle block.
spawn WindowProvider::Window main
main.Create(1024, 768, 'ETCS 3D scene -- WASD')

main.spawn(RenderProvider::Surface view)
view.Create(@gpu)

# ── the 2D side: a compositor, so UI and the 3D view merge in one buffer ──
spawn RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D stage
stage.Create(1024, 768)
stage.SetPosition(0, 0)
stage.SetBackground(0.04, 0.05, 0.08, 1.0)

# ── the camera: a Drawable2D that a scene fills ──────────────────────────
#
# Nested in the stage like any other 2D node, positioned in the stage's space
# like any other 2D node, ordered against its siblings like any other 2D node.
# It is a camera only in what fills it.
stage.spawn(RenderProvider::Camera3D eye)
eye.Create(1024, 768)
eye.SetPosition(0, 0)
eye.SetOrder(1)
eye.SetBackground(0.07, 0.09, 0.15, 1.0)
eye.LookAt(0.0, 3.0, -14.0, 0.0, 0.5, 2.0)
eye.SetLens(60.0, 0.1, 400.0)

# ── the scene: an invisible root, and the geometry under it ──────────────
#
# The root is a container rather than a box you can see: it is the coordinate
# origin the scene is stated against and the single thing w/a/s/d translates.
# Making it invisible is not a trick -- a container with no appearance is
# exactly what a parent-relative tree wants at its top, and the 2D tree's root
# rectangle is the same node with SetVisible left on.
spawn RenderProvider::Scene3D world
world.Create(1.0, 1.0, 1.0)
world.SetVisible(0)
world.SetPosition(0.0, 0.0, 0.0)
# Terminal speed and drag, per second -- held keys are an acceleration, not a
# displacement, so these two are the whole feel. Raise the damping for crisper
# stops, lower it to coast.
world.SetSpeed(7.0)
world.SetDamping(6.0)
# THE LOOK IS ABSOLUTE: where the pointer sits on the camera's frame IS the
# direction the camera looks. The frame is the plane bisecting the view cone,
# so its bounding box is the field of view, and the pointer's offset from the
# centre of that box is a position on a rotational axis -- the full circle
# across the width, and the pitch range down the height.
#
# SENSITIVITY IS THE YAW SPAN. 1.0 means the width is exactly one turn. It does
# NOT touch pitch: only a circle can be gone round twice inside one frame, and a
# bounded axis compressed the same way just puts its own stops inside the frame,
# which is a dead zone. The height maps onto the pitch range once, top edge to
# bottom edge, always. 2.0 -- the default, and what is set here -- makes
# the width TWO turns, which is the more usable choice: a half turn, the thing
# you ask a look for most often, then lands a quarter of the width from centre
# rather than at the very edge, so the pointer stays near the middle of the
# frame. The axis is compressed, not clipped: every direction is still
# reachable, twice over. Smaller stretches it back out. This is the number to
# experiment with; there is no other.
#
# NOTHING ACCUMULATES, which is the point. Put the pointer back where it was
# and the view is exactly back where it was -- no drift, no lag, nothing to
# reset. It also needs no cursor locking, so it behaves the same on displays
# that refuse to let an application move the pointer (Qubes, XWayland, remote
# X), where a delta-based look silently turns the whole screen into a joystick.
#
# world.Look() prints the angles and both spans.
world.SetSensitivity(2.0)

# Heat leaves a point into whatever contains it, and the root's environment is
# outside the model -- so this is the rate at which the scene's accumulated
# drag heat stops being the scene's problem (ontology/OrderVector.h). Read the
# ledger back at any time with world.Order().
world.SetEmissivity(0.5)

run RenderProvider/scripts/scene3d.etcs anchor=world

# One call, and the direction of causality: the camera names the scene. The
# scene does not know it is being watched until it is projected, at which point
# it records the viewer so a later move can mark it (Scene3D.h).
eye.SetScene(@world)

# ── 2D UI, drawn ON the camera's frame ───────────────────────────────────
#
# Children of the camera, in the camera's own space, drawn after the
# projection and clipped to the frame. This is the composition the Camera
# family exists for: the 3D view is the main surface and the 2D tree is what
# sits on top of it, with no adapter between the two because there is no
# difference between the two.
# A live frame counter over the 3D view -- a Glyphs leaf nested in the camera
# exactly like the reticle below, so it is drawn after the projection and
# clipped to the frame. "%f" is substituted at draw time from the surface named
# by BindFps, because the rate is a property of the frame loop and a script
# cannot run per frame (TextLabel::BindFps).
#
# IT COSTS FRAMES, and that is not a bug to hide: a label whose text changes
# every frame is a self-animating node, so it answers yes to Animating() and
# keeps every compositor above it recomposing. A settled scene with the counter
# on runs at roughly two thirds of what it runs at with the counter off --
# which is the honest price of watching the number, and the reason to take it
# off once you have read it.
eye.spawn(RenderProvider::TextLabel hud)
hud.Create(24)
hud.SetText(%f fps -- wasd move / mouse look / ctrl+tab)
hud.SetPosition(16, 16)
hud.SetColor(0.6, 1.0, 0.7, 1.0)
hud.SetBackground(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.45)
hud.SetPadding(6)
hud.SetOrder(11)
hud.BindFps(@view)

eye.spawn(RenderProvider::PolygonDrawable2D reticle)
reticle.Create()
reticle.AddPoint(506, 378)
reticle.AddPoint(518, 378)
reticle.AddPoint(518, 390)
reticle.AddPoint(506, 390)
reticle.SetFill(1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 0.55)
reticle.SetOrder(10)

# ── the frame edge ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Compose, not Draw. Draw would issue one composition now and the surface would
# replay it forever -- right for a still picture, and exactly wrong for a tree
# that moves, since replaying past calls never asks the camera to make a new
# image. Binding the root makes the frame edge re-walk it each tick, which is
# where every dirty flag in this system finally pays for itself: a frame with
# nothing moving costs one DrawInto per node, no projection, and no upload.
view.Clear(0.02, 0.02, 0.03, 1.0)
view.Compose(@stage)

# Hides the cursor, and that is all it does now -- no grab, no confinement, no
# warping. An absolute look has nothing to confine: the pointer's position over
# the frame is the angle, so it never needs somewhere to keep going. This is
# also why the control behaves identically on displays that refuse to let an
# application move the pointer.
#
# CTRL+TAB is the way out -- handled by the window itself (GLFWWindow.h), so it
# still works if whatever is consuming the input does not.
main.CaptureMouse(1)

detach scene3d_input.etcs window=main scene=world
detach RenderProvider/scripts/render_frames.etcs view=view

# does nothing but final setup and run for the window + cleanup when Run exits
detach scene3d_profile.etcs main=main
